"use strict";(globalThis.webpackChunklearnalgorithms=globalThis.webpackChunklearnalgorithms||[]).push([[7484],{8970(e){e.exports=JSON.parse('{"tag":{"label":"Proofs","permalink":"/concepts/tags/proofs","allTagsPath":"/concepts/tags","count":5,"items":[{"id":"state-space-search-and-optimization/proofs","title":"Proofs: Backtracking and Branch and Bound","description":"Backtracking and branch and bound are exact methods: they promise to find every solution, or the optimal one. That promise depends on the pruning being safe, that is, never discarding a branch that contains what we are looking for. This page proves when that is the case, and proves the specific facts used in the examples.","permalink":"/concepts/state-space-search-and-optimization/proofs"},{"id":"algorithm-design-paradigms/proofs","title":"Proofs: Design Paradigms","description":"This page proves the claims of the chapter: the formulas used to analyze divide and conquer, the correctness and cost of several divide and conquer algorithms, the optimality of the greedy algorithms that the lessons call optimal, and the recurrences of dynamic programming. The general techniques (induction, recursion trees, geometric series) are in the Mathematical Background chapter.","permalink":"/concepts/algorithm-design-paradigms/proofs"},{"id":"foundations-of-algorithms/proofs","title":"Proofs: Foundations","description":"This page collects the proofs behind the claims of the chapter. The lessons state the results and use them; here they are justified. Each section is self-contained, and the lessons link to the section they need. The proof techniques (induction and loop invariants) are explained in Induction, Correctness and Recurrences.","permalink":"/concepts/foundations-of-algorithms/proofs"},{"id":"advanced-execution-models/proofs","title":"Proofs: Parallel Algorithms","description":"This page derives the formulas of the performance lesson and proves that the Fork/Join scheme computes the same result as the sequential algorithm. Times are measured relative to the sequential time, which we take as 1 unless stated otherwise.","permalink":"/concepts/advanced-execution-models/proofs"},{"id":"fundamental-problem-domains/proofs","title":"Proofs: Sorting","description":"This page proves the claims made in the sorting lessons: that each algorithm sorts correctly, the costs given in the lessons, and the limits that no comparison-based algorithm can break. The proofs use loop invariants and induction, explained in Induction, Correctness and Recurrences. Arrays are indexed from 0 and have $n$ elements.","permalink":"/concepts/fundamental-problem-domains/proofs"}],"unlisted":false}}')}}]);